WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.607
Correlation gives us a numerical summary of the linear relationship between two numeric variables.

00:00:06.607 --> 00:00:11.072
Pearson correlation ranges from minus one to plus one.

00:00:11.072 --> 00:00:15.402
A positive value means the variables tend to increase together.

00:00:15.402 --> 00:00:20.072
A negative value means one tends to decrease as the other increases.

00:00:20.072 --> 00:00:24.058
A value near zero means there is little linear association.

00:00:24.687 --> 00:00:29.414
Run the code and compare the coefficient with the scatter plot from the previous slide.

00:00:29.414 --> 00:00:31.779
The word linear is important.

00:00:31.779 --> 00:00:36.274
A correlation near zero does not prove that there is no relationship.

00:00:36.274 --> 00:00:41.599
The relationship could be curved or affected by other structure in the data.

00:00:41.599 --> 00:00:45.394
Also remember that correlation is not causation.

00:00:45.394 --> 00:00:48.679
It describes association in the observed data.

00:00:48.679 --> 00:00:53.645
It does not prove that changing one variable will cause the other variable to change.
